px;"><div class=3D"markdown-body"><p>A <strong>lifecycle</strong> is the to=
p level of the three-level transaction model=20
(<strong>Lifecycle =E2=86=92 Card Transaction =E2=86=92 Transaction Event</=
strong>).</p>
<p>It is a container that groups all related card transactions and transact=
ion events belonging to=20
the same payment flow, based on network information. Lifecycles provide a n=
etwork-level view=20
of a payment along with aggregated billing amounts across all related card =
transactions and events.</p>
<p><strong>Key characteristics:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Lifecycles do not have a status. Use card transactions if you need guar=
anteed terminal statuses.</li>
<li>Grouping is <strong>best-effort</strong>: it depends on payment network=
s and acquirers providing consistent=20
identifiers. For example, matched refunds link to the original lifecycle; u=
nmatched refunds=20
create a new one.</li>
<li>Use the lifecycle ID to retrieve all associated card transactions and t=
ransaction events.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Data retention:</strong> Resources can be queried for a maximum =
of 2 years from creation.</p>
<p>Learn more about the <a href=3D"https://www.airwallex.com/docs/issuing/t=
ransactions/transaction-lifecycle" style=3D"color: #612FFF; font-weight: 50=
0; overflow-wrap: break-word;">transaction lifecycle</a>.</p>
